Stateless.

Each frame, for this loop index:
```
const n = clamp(ctx.inputCount, 2, 32);
let on = true;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  if (!ctx.input(`value${i}`)) { on = false; break; }
}
ctx.output("output", on);
```

- Coercion: each input is coerced to boolean by core (numbers are on when `> 0`, text "true"/"yes"/"on" is on, vectors use their first component).
- Pulses in, state out: inputs are booleans, so a pulse arriving from upstream reads `true` only on the frame it fires. The output is a boolean, not a pulse. A downstream pulse input fires on its false→true edge, so two pulses on consecutive frames merge into one downstream pulse (the output stays true for two frames with no false in between).
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values and outputs on the same frame. There is no latency and no history.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. Output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. An empty loop gives an empty output.
- Edge cases: `inputCount` outside 2–32 (a hand-edited file) is clamped into that range; missing expanded inputs read their default `false`.
- Coming from Origami: unnamed ports import positionally (port 0 → `value1`, port 1 → `value2`, …). The file's input count becomes `inputCount`.

