Stateless.

Each frame, for this loop index:
```
const a = ctx.input("value1");            // number, or number[] for vector variants
const b = ctx.input("value2");
const tolerance = Math.abs(ctx.input("tolerance"));
const av = typeof a === "number" ? [a] : a;
const bv = typeof b === "number" ? [b] : b;
let sum = 0, scale = 1;
for (let i = 0; i < av.length; i++) {
  const d = av[i] - bv[i];
  sum += d * d;
  scale = Math.max(scale, Math.abs(av[i]), Math.abs(bv[i]));
}
const distance = Math.sqrt(sum);            // |a − b| for numbers, Euclidean for vectors
ctx.output("output", distance <= tolerance + 1e-9 * scale);
```

- Boundary: inclusive. The `1e-9 · max(1, |components|)` slack absorbs floating-point error so documented cases hold: `2.1` vs `2` with tolerance `0.1` is true, even though `2.1 − 2` is `0.10000000000000009` in doubles.
- Vectors: `point`, `point3d`, `point4d`, and `size` compare by Euclidean distance across all components, so a tolerance of 10 means "within a 10 pt circle".
- Edge cases: a negative tolerance uses its absolute value. A tolerance of 0 still applies the tiny slack, so `0.1 + 0.2` equals `0.3`. Inputs arrive finite (core coercion turns NaN and ±Infinity into 0); treat any non-finite value that slips through as 0.
- Muted: output is false (the inputs are variant or number, so nothing passes through).
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values and outputs on the same frame. There is no latency and no history.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. Output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. An empty loop gives an empty output.
- Coming from Origami: `First Value` → `value1`, `Second Value` → `value2`, `Tolerance` → `tolerance`. Files store the id as `builtin.compare.equalApprox`. Origami files always import as the `number` variant.

Stateless.

Each frame, for this loop index:
```
const n = clamp(ctx.inputCount, 2, 32);
const toNum = (v) => (typeof v === "boolean" ? (v ? 1 : 0) : v);
let ok = true;
for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
  const a = toNum(ctx.input(`value${i}`));
  const b = toNum(ctx.input(`value${i + 1}`));
  if (!(a > b)) { ok = false; break; }
}
ctx.output("output", ok);
```

- Chain: each value is compared with the **next** one, and every pair must pass: `value1 > value2` AND `value2 > value3` AND …. So `3 > 2 > 1` is true and `4 > 3 > 10` is false. This is not "Value 1 against every other value": `10 > 3 > 4` is false even though Value 1 passes against Value 3.
- Strict: equal neighbors fail, so two unconnected inputs (0 and 0) output false.
- Types: `number` compares numerically with no tolerance (`-0` equals `0`). `index` compares integers. `boolean` treats false as 0 and true as 1.
- Pulses: a pulse wired into an input reads 1 (number, index) or true (boolean) only on its frame.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values and outputs on the same frame. There is no latency and no history.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. Output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. An empty loop gives an empty output.
- Edge cases: `inputCount` outside 2–32 is clamped; missing expanded inputs read 0. Inputs arrive finite because core coercion turns NaN and ±Infinity into 0; treat any non-finite value that slips through as 0.
- Muted: output is false.
- Coming from Origami: unnamed ports import positionally (port 0 → `value1`, port 1 → `value2`, …), and Change Type maps to `typeParam`. Every added port counts (Origami fixed a bug where extra ports were ignored in v121). Comparison patches can be swapped for one another with Replace With and keep their connections, because they share port keys.

