Stateless.

Each frame, for this loop index:
```
const v = ctx.input("value");
let lo = ctx.input("min"), hi = ctx.input("max");
if (lo > hi) [lo, hi] = [hi, lo];
const bounds = ctx.input("bounds");
const includeLo = bounds === "includeBoth" || bounds === "includeMin";
const includeHi = bounds === "includeBoth" || bounds === "includeMax";
const inside = (includeLo ? v >= lo : v > lo) && (includeHi ? v <= hi : v < hi);
const below = !inside && (v < lo || (v === lo && !includeLo));
ctx.output("inRange", inside);
ctx.output("below", below);
ctx.output("above", !inside && !below);
```

- Exactly one output is true every frame.
- Edges: an excluded lower edge counts as Below, and an excluded upper edge counts as Above.
- `min > max`: the two are swapped, so Min 10 and Max 0 behave like Min 0 and Max 10. Bounds then applies to the lower and upper edges after swapping.
- Zero-width range (`min == max`): with Include Both, only that exact value is in range. With Include Min, Include Max, or Exclude Both, nothing is in range; a value equal to the edge is Above for Include Min and Below for Include Max and Exclude Both.
- No tolerance: comparisons use exact doubles. Inputs arrive finite because core coercion turns NaN and ±Infinity into 0; treat any non-finite value that slips through as 0.
- Unknown `bounds` keys (a hand-edited file) behave as `includeBoth`.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values and outputs on the same frame. There is no latency and no history.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. Output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. An empty loop gives an empty output.
- Coming from Origami: no import mapping. Origami builds this with Greater Than or Equal and Less Than or Equal combined through And (or one three-port Less Than or Equal chain, `min ≤ value ≤ max`).
